e) ?? 0) + 1);\n    } else {\n      basePremium += basePremiumFor(item.type);\n    }\n  }\n  for (const count of componentCounts.values()) {\n    basePremium += componentGroupBasePremium(count);\n  }\n  const firstInsuranceSurcharge = basePremium * FIRST_INSURANCE_SURCHARGE_RATE;\n  return PROCESSING_FEE + basePremium + firstInsuranceSurcharge;\n};\n\nexport const runScenario = (scenario: Scenario): ScenarioResult => {\n  const results: StepResult[] = scenario.steps.map((step) =>\n    step.op === \"quote\"\n      ? { premium: quotePremium(step.items) }\n      : { premium: PROCESSING_FEE }\n  );\n  return { results };\n};\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784908481755},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Good, this is clean. The first-insurance-per-policy vs per-item distinction: since first insurance is 10% of total base and each item is treated as first insurance (10% each), applying 10% to the sum equals applying 10% per item and summing. Mathematically identical. So this is fine.\n\n## Red -- Test 8: 4 runes -> 100 G base premium (no block)\n\n4 runes = 1 block (60) + 1 single (25) = 85 base. Newcomer: 85 + 8.5 + 5 = 98.5 → but wait, spec says 4 runes → 100 G base premium (no block — block requires exactly 3).\n\nThe spec says \"no block\" for 4! Not \"1 block + 1 single\". Let me re-read: \"4 runes → 100 G base premium (no block — block requires exactly 3)\". So a block requires *exactly* 3, meaning 4 runes = 4×25 = 100, no block at all. My current impl gives 85. This test should fail."},{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_vrtx_017c98RBGpwb9P25XMKWDLwJ","name":"edit","arguments":{"edits":[{"newText":"  it(\"4 runes -> 100 G base premium (no block, requires exactly 3)\", () => {\n    const out = runScenario({\n      customer: { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 },\n      steps: [\n        {\n          op: \"quote\",\n          items: [{ type: \"rune\" }, { type: \"rune\" }, { type: \"rune\" }, { type: \"rune\" }],\n        },\n      ],\n    });\n    // no block: 4*2