eed to think through whether extracting them into a separate structure would actually improve readability or just add complexity.\n\nOne clear win is extracting the loyalty eligibility check into a named helper function like `isLoyalCustomer`. That makes the intent much more obvious than the inline condition. Let me calculate the cognitive mass of the current `quotePremium` function to see if there are other improvements worth making. Extracting a helper function to check loyalty status makes the intent clearer, and even though it adds an invocation, the improved readability justifies it. Let me go ahead with this refactoring.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"All 19 tests pass. Now let me analyze for refactoring following the Four Rules of Simple Design in priority order.\n\n## Step 1: Naming Evaluation (FIRST PRIORITY)\n\n- `quotePremium(items, customer)` — clearly reveals intent (computes the quote premium). ✓\n- `sumItemPremiums`, `itemSurcharge`, `componentGroupBasePremium`, `isComponent`, `basePremiumFor` — all descriptive. ✓\n- **Gap found**: The loyalty eligibility check `customer.yearsWithMHPCO >= LOYALTY_YEARS_THRESHOLD` is an inline conditional expressing a domain concept (\"is this a long-standing/loyal customer?\"). This bare comparison does not reveal its *intent* at the call site.\n\n**Decision**: Extract `isLoyalCustomer(customer)` to reveal domain intent (Rule 2).\n\n## Step 2: Initial APP Mass (`quotePremium`)\n\n```\nconst quotePremium = (items, customer) => {\n  const { base, surcharges } = sumItemPremiums(items);\n  const firstInsuranceSurcharge = base * FIRST_INSURANCE_SURCHARGE_RATE;\n  const loyaltyDiscount =\n    customer.yearsWithMHPCO >= LOYALTY_YEARS_THRESHOLD ? base * LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE : 0;\n  return PROCESSING_FEE + base + surcharges + firstInsuranceSurcharge - loyaltyDiscount;\n};\n```\n- Bindings: items, customer, base, surcharges, firstInsuranceSurcharge, loyaltyDiscount, + property refs (yearsWithMHPCO) ≈ 7 → 7\n- Constants: `0` = 1 → 1\n- Invocations: `sumItemPremiums`, `*`, `>=`, `*`, and `+ + + -` (4) = 8 → 16\n- Conditionals: 1 ternary → 4\n- **Total Mass ≈ 28**\n\n## Step 3–4: Apply Rule 2 (Reveals Intent)\n\nRules 1 (tests pass ✓), then Rule 2 is the highest-value improvement. Extracting `isLoyalCustomer` names the domain concept."},{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_vrtx_01Fx96dTu3L4VPH1e1FJCwvj","name":"edit","arguments":{"path":"/home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-24_15-48-37_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_opus-4-8-no-thinking/src/claim-office.ts","edits":[{"oldText":"const quotePremium = (items: Item[], customer: Customer): number => {\n  const { base, surcharges } = sumItemPremiums(items);\n  const firstInsuranceSurcharge = base * FIRST_INSURANCE_SURCHARGE_RATE;\n  const loyaltyDiscount =\n    customer.yearsWithMHPCO >= LOYALTY_YEARS_THRESHOLD ? base * LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE : 0;\n  return PROCESSING_FEE + base + surcharges + firstInsuranceSurcharge - loyaltyDiscount;\n};","newText":"const isLoyalCustomer = (customer: Customer): boolean =>\n  customer.yearsWithMHPCO >= LOYALTY_YEARS_THRESHOLD;\n\nconst quotePremium = (items: Item[], customer: Customer): number => {\n  const { base, surcharges } = sumItemPremiums(items);\n  const firstInsuranceSurcharge = base * FIRST_INSURANCE_SURCHARGE_RATE;\n  const loyaltyDiscount = isLoyalCustomer(customer) ? base * LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE : 0;\n  return PROCESSING_FEE + base + surcharges + firstInsuranceSurcharge - loyaltyDiscount;\n};"}]}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"vertex/claude-opus-4-8@eu","usage":{"input":6977,"output":2754,"cacheRead":9704,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":0,"totalTokens":19435,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784908841993,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-99d04c8f-6994-4723-95e6-66be4a83fdab","responseModel":"claude-opus-4-8"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"toolu_vrtx_01F