w*.\n\nLet me refactor that.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"All 22 tests pass. Now let me apply the refactoring process.\n\n## Refactoring Analysis\n\n### Step 1: Naming Evaluation (FIRST PRIORITY)\n\nI reviewed all function names:\n- `quotePremium`, `sumItemPremiums`, `totalDiscount`, `itemSurcharge`, `componentGroupBasePremium`, `isComponent`, `basePremiumFor`, `isLoyalCustomer` — all clearly reveal intent.\n\nOne issue: the recent Green phase added a bare `Math.ceil(premium)`. The **why** (rounding up is deliberately in *MHPCO's favor*, per the spec) is invisible at the call site. `Math.ceil` reveals the mechanism but not the intent.\n\n**Decision**: Extract a named helper `roundPremiumInMHPCOFavor` to make the domain rule self-documenting (Rule 2: Reveals Intent).\n\n### Step 2: Initial APP Mass (focus: `quotePremium`)\n\n```\nconst premium = PROCESSING_FEE + base + surcharges + firstInsuranceSurcharge - totalDiscount(base, context);\nreturn Math.ceil(premium);\n```\n- Bindings: items, context, base, surcharges, firstInsuranceSurcharge, premium = 6\n- Invocations: sumItemPremiums, totalDiscount, Math.ceil, `+`×4, `-`, `*` = ~9 → 18\n- Assignments (const bindings via destructuring/let-free): treated as bindings above\n\nLocal mass of the return/rounding logic ≈ bindings(6) + invocations for arithmetic. The bare `Math.ceil` adds no clarity mass.\n\n### Step 3–4: Apply Rule 2"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_vrtx_019SzDg2echRgGckwUGdJT74","name":"edit","arguments":{"path":"src/claim-office.ts","edits":[{"oldText":"const quotePremium = (items: Item[], context: QuoteContext): number => {\n  const { base, surcharges } = sumItemPremiums(items);\n  const firstInsuranceSurcharge = base * FIRST_INSURANCE_SURCHARGE_RATE;\n  const premium =\n    PROCESSING_FEE + base + surcharges + firstInsuranceSurcharge - totalDiscount(base, context);\n  return Math.ceil(premium);\n};","newText":"// Premiums round up so any fractional Gold falls in MHPCO'